        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Hastings</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">Saturday 14 October 1066, from about 9 am to dusk</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">About 7 miles (11 km) northwest of Hastings, close to the present-day town of Battle, East Sussex, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>; sources differ on the exact site of the English line</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">A Norman-French army under William, Duke of Normandy, against an English army under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>; numbers unknown, with modern estimates of 7000 to 8000 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> among several, and most historians arguing for 7000 to 8000 English</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold</a> was killed late in the battle, most of his army retreated or was defeated, and William went on to be crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 25 December 1066.</span></div>
        </div>
