    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> died childless in January 1066, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold</a> had to face three rival claimants, Hardrada and Tostig in the north and William in the south, within a few weeks.</li><li>The English fought on foot in a shield wall; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, infantry and cavalry, and probably pretended to flee in order to break the English line.</li><li>The battle is known from conflicting accounts, so that even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold</a>'s death, by an arrow in the eye in the traditional version, remains uncertain.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Although later lists of William's companions exist, only about 35 named individuals can reliably be identified as having been with him at Hastings, and only about 20 named Englishmen with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

